> I am not sure if I am doing something wrong. Here is the Godot 3 project version if you want to try it yourself:

Firstly, just an important point that I wanted to clarify as it predictably causes confusion:

**The first thing to learn about physics Interpolation is it nothing to do with physics** :grinning: 

The correct name is fixed timestep interpolation, the only relation to physics is that the timesteps coincide with the physics engine ticks in Godot.
https://docs.godotengine.org/en/3.6/tutorials/physics/interpolation/physics_interpolation_introduction.html#fixed-timestep-interpolation

Therefore it's far easier to examine if MRPs do not include any physics (simplify and eliminate anything complex that may be causing secondary effects). So here my first step was to simplify the project to remove all physics objects and just use `Spatials` with a fixed movement speed in `_physics_process()`:

[Test Physics Interpolation Init2.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/15595957/Test.Physics.Interpolation.Init2.zip)

That said, there _does_ appear to be a bug (even in 3.6), and it is likely order of operations (in fact I'm pretty sure I had to fix exactly an equivalent of this recently somewhere else in the interpolation).

The problem is that in Godot if you call `set_position()`, Godot defers that to some point in the future where it flushes the transforms (lazy updating optimization), and if you call `reset_physics_interpolation()` before the deferred change has gone through the wrong transform gets reset.

The good news is that these are usually pretty simple to fix, I'll take a look tomorrow. :+1: 

UPDATE:
Yes as I suspected it's just the `VisualServer` transform being out of date because of lazy updating.
This fixes it in `VisualInstance`:
```
		case NOTIFICATION_RESET_PHYSICS_INTERPOLATION: {
			if (_is_vi_visible() && is_physics_interpolated()) {
				// We must ensure the VisualServer transform is up to date before resetting.
				// This is because NOTIFICATION_TRANSFORM_CHANGED is deferred,
				// and cannot be relied to be called in order before NOTIFICATION_RESET_PHYSICS_INTERPOLATION.
				if (!_is_using_identity_transform()) {
					VisualServer::get_singleton()->instance_set_transform(instance, get_global_transform());
				}

				VisualServer::get_singleton()->instance_reset_physics_interpolation(instance);
			}
```
I'll be adding this to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/92784 which should get forward ported to this PR.

> but get_global_transform_interpolated() still returns values that are not interpolated.

I've just debugged for this PR and you are indeed correct, while this is working in 3.x, the physics seems to be updating the transform in 4.x _before_ `SceneTree::physics_process()` is called.

We can try and work out why this is, it could be due to e.g. transforms being flushed prior to `SceneTree::physics_process()`.

EDIT: Yes it's likely the sync calls before this. I'll try pumping the client interpolation data before the sync calls see if that fixes it. This works differently in 3.x I suspect, hence porting same code doesn't work.

UPDATE: Yes this works, @rburing I've moved the `_client_physics_interpolation.physics_process()` to occur in `iteration_prepare()`:
```
void SceneTree::iteration_prepare() {
	if (_physics_interpolation_enabled) {
		// Make sure any pending transforms from the last tick / frame
		// are flushed before pumping the interpolation prev and currents.
		flush_transform_notifications();
		RenderingServer::get_singleton()->tick();
		
#ifndef _3D_DISABLED
		// Any objects performing client physics interpolation
		// should be given an opportunity to keep their previous transforms
		// up to take before each new physics tick.
		_client_physics_interpolation.physics_process();
#endif
	}
}
```
I'll have a look whether it would be worth adjusting this position in 3.x too to keep master and 3.x in sync. Certainly I don't think 3.x has been tested so much with this combination of 3d and 2d dependency as in the MRP.

